Security and Risk Management Module Reflection – Shamma Alshamsi

The Security and Risk Management (SRM_PCOM7E) module has been an insightful and transformative learning experience that expanded both my technical knowledge and professional competencies. Engaging with the core themes of cybersecurity, digital risks, business continuity, and threat modelling, I not only gained theoretical frameworks but also applied them in realistic scenarios through group collaboration, individual assignments, and coding-based tasks. This reflection aims to evaluate my learning journey through four lenses: what I learned, how I learned it, who I learned with, and how this knowledge will be applied in my future career.

Initially, the foundation was laid in Units 1 to 5, which covered the essentials of risk, threats, and vulnerabilities. One of the most valuable realisations was understanding the importance of aligning cybersecurity strategies with organisational goals, rather than viewing them as standalone technical functions. Concepts like threat actors, risk appetite, confidentiality-integrity-availability (CIA) triad, and asset valuation laid the groundwork for analysing threats systematically (Bayuk et al., 2012). I appreciated how case-based learning in the module allowed us to contextualise these principles. For example, our group applied the STRIDE threat model and the NIST framework when evaluating the Pampered Pets case, which simulated real-world business scenarios (Shostack, 2014).

A significant milestone was reached in Unit 6 during the Development Team Project: Risk Identification Report. Working collaboratively, our team identified several operational vulnerabilities facing Pampered Pets, including lack of network segmentation, insecure API endpoints, and outdated antivirus software. I led the analysis of insider threats and social engineering risks. This sharpened my attention to human factors in cybersecurity, which are often overlooked but remain one of the most exploited vulnerabilities in real organisations (Hadnagy, 2018). This experience also reinforced the importance of threat intelligence and system mapping. We created risk matrices that classified risks based on their likelihood and impact, which directly informed our mitigation recommendations.

The “how” of learning throughout this module was equally important. I learned best by doing—whether through weekly scenario-based activities, coding exercises using Python for encryption simulations, or visualising threats via attack trees and flowcharts. For instance, in the coding segment of the e-portfolio, I tested sample code snippets to simulate brute force attempts and basic input sanitisation techniques using Python. These exercises not only developed my understanding of common exploits like SQL injection and buffer overflow but also helped me appreciate the link between software development and secure architecture (Kim & Solomon, 2021). The clarity offered by running test cases and observing different outputs was immensely satisfying, providing immediate feedback and deepening conceptual understanding.

Moreover, this module introduced me to the value of peer collaboration. Through the Peer Evaluation Template, I received constructive feedback on communication, timeliness, contribution, and leadership. I scored high in team commitment, which I take pride in, as it demonstrates my reliability and respect for shared deadlines. However, a key area for improvement was technical depth in infrastructure-related risks. Recognising this gap, I proactively researched concepts like zero-trust architecture and role-based access control (RBAC), which I later integrated into the Individual Project: Executive Summary. This self-correction and growth mindset was a direct result of open team dialogue and our strong team ethic.

Completing the Executive Summary further consolidated my individual learning. Tasked with proposing a cybersecurity strategy for a fictional UAE-based e-commerce platform, I applied ISO 27005 and NIST SP 800-30 as the guiding risk frameworks (ISACA, 2020). I outlined a three-pronged risk treatment plan involving preventive controls, detection systems, and recovery processes. The most rewarding part of this assignment was developing a business-focused rationale for each security recommendation. For example, proposing multi-factor authentication (MFA) wasn’t just a technical upgrade—it had to be justified in terms of cost, ease of adoption, and customer experience. This helped me bridge the gap between technical cybersecurity measures and executive decision-making, which is critical for strategic alignment (Von Solms & Van Niekerk, 2013).

Another major learning point was understanding disaster recovery (DR) and business continuity planning (BCP). Unit 9 exposed me to tools like BIA (Business Impact Analysis), RPO (Recovery Point Objective), and RTO (Recovery Time Objective). These helped me assess how long an organisation could afford to be offline and how often backups should be taken. I found this especially relevant for UAE institutions like banks and government portals that operate 24/7. My future ambition is to work in a cybersecurity leadership role, and mastering these resilience concepts will undoubtedly prove useful.

Throughout the module, I also gained proficiency in documentation and standards compliance. All our group work and individual reports adhered to GDPR, ISO, and NIST regulations—something I had limited experience with before. Understanding the implications of data protection regulations has been instrumental in shaping my ethical awareness as an IT professional (Whitman & Mattord, 2021). Furthermore, participating in the e-portfolio structure made me more organised and detail-oriented. I learned to evidence my learning outcomes by linking practical tasks with theoretical concepts and professional reflections, which mirrored workplace audit and compliance processes.

Perhaps the most significant transformation has been in my professional confidence. I now feel more prepared to manage cybersecurity risks, write risk assessments, communicate with stakeholders, and defend the rationale behind technical decisions. I’ve become more comfortable using Python for security automation, testing API security, and conducting risk simulations using attack-defense trees. The ability to apply theory in meaningful ways has strengthened both my employability and leadership potential.

Moving forward, I plan to apply the skills from this module to real workplace challenges in the UAE. Whether it is conducting penetration testing, designing awareness campaigns for end-users, or auditing systems for compliance, the learning from SRM_PCOM7E has provided me with a strong foundation. I also intend to pursue further certifications such as CompTIA Security+ or ISO 27001 Lead Implementer, building on the competencies acquired here.

In conclusion, this module has not only enhanced my academic understanding of cybersecurity and risk management but has also cultivated essential workplace skills such as communication, problem-solving, strategic thinking, and ethical awareness. I am grateful for the support of my tutors, teammates, and the practical structure of the module that encouraged deep learning. I look forward to transferring this knowledge into real-world impact in my career as a cybersecurity professional.

